          deepDive: "**〜のなんのって** is a lively spoken way to say *so ~ you wouldn't believe it*. The book's gloss: 言葉でうまく説明できないくらい非常に〜だ, *so extreme I can't put it into words*. Literally it's like *\"~, or what, or whatever…\"*: the speaker names the quality and then gives up looking for a word big enough, which conveys the degree.\n\nTwo shapes:\n- **X のなんのって、[vivid consequence]**: うるさいのなんのって、全然寝られなかった (*it was unbelievably noisy; I couldn't sleep at all*), 疲れたのなんのって、最後はもう一歩も歩けないっていう感じだった. The second half proves the degree.\n- **X のなんのって。** The sentence just stops and the listener fills in: 痛いのなんのって。(*it hurt like crazy*), びっくりしたのなんのって。(*I was so shocked, you have no idea*).\n\nConnection: **plain form + のなんのって**: い-adjectives (痛い, 多い, 忙しかった), verbs, typically in た-form (びっくりした, 疲れた), and な-adjectives with な (静かなのなんのって). The book's bracket crosses out N＋だ: nouns aren't used. Rephrase with an adjective or verb: ✗人気なのなんのって → ✓人気があるのなんのって.\n\nNuance and register:\n- Strictly casual, conversational and a little exaggerated. It narrates the speaker's own experience or impression, usually something overwhelming or unpleasant (痛い, うるさい, 忙しい, 疲れた), but positive things work too, as in the sample: メニューが多いのなんのって (*the menu was just enormous*).\n- It looks back on something experienced; it isn't used for predictions, requests or plans.\n- In writing or formal speech, use 非常に, 言葉にできないほど, or a pattern like 〜てならない.\n\nContrast:\n- **〜ことか** (#42): どんなに / どれほど + ことか is an emotional exclamation, often a monologue: どれほど心配したことか (*how worried I was!*). のなんのって is chattier and is usually followed by the evidence.\n- **〜てならない** (#121): *extremely ~*, for feelings that well up inside (心配でならない *I can't help worrying*). Written-leaning and about inner feelings, not about noise or crowds.\n- **〜といったらない** (N1): *indescribably ~*, similar exaggeration but also used in writing.\n- **〜くらい / 〜ほど**: neutral degree: トイレに行くひまもないくらい忙しかった.\n\nCommon mistakes:\n- Putting a noun before it: ✗雨なのなんのって → ✓雨がひどいのなんのって.\n- Using it in a report or an email to a superior.\n\nJLPT tip: in 問題3 casual texts (blogs, messages), a blank right after an emotion or adjective followed by a striking scene, like びっくりした［1］、一面の銀世界, points to のなんのって; a reason (からだ) or contrast (けれど, ところが) doesn't fit the flow.",
