          deepDive: "**〜たて** means *freshly ~ed, just ~ed*: the action has just been completed and its result is still new. The book: 〜が完了して、〜になってすぐの状態. 焼きたてのパン = *bread fresh out of the oven*; ペンキぬりたて = *wet paint*.\n\nConnection: **V-ます stem + たて**: 焼き → 焼きたて, 炊き → 炊きたて, でき → できたて. The result behaves like a noun:\n- **〜たての + N**: 炊きたてのご飯 (*freshly cooked rice*), 覚えたての歌 (*a song she just learned*).\n- **〜たてだ／です**: このベンチ、ペンキぬりたてだって (*this bench has just been painted*).\n- with particles: できたてを買ってきた (*I bought them fresh*).\n\nWhich verbs? The ＊ note lists the core ones (作る・できる・焼く・炊く・なる), and in practice たて lives in a small set of collocations:\n- Food and drink: 焼きたて, 炊きたて, 揚げたて, 作りたて, できたて, とれたて (*freshly picked or caught*), 搾りたて, いれたて (*freshly brewed*).\n- A new status or skill: 社会人になりたて (*just started working*), 入社したての新人, 覚えたての言葉. The person is still new at it, sometimes with a hint of inexperience.\n- Signs: ペンキ塗りたて.\n\nNuance: with food and things, たて praises freshness; with people it simply notes newness. It doesn't combine with verbs whose result isn't a \"fresh state\": ✗行きたて, ✗読みたて. 買いたて is heard only colloquially; the standard form is 買ったばかり.\n\nContrast:\n- **〜かけ** = *half-done, started but not finished*: 書きかけのレポート (*a half-written report*), 飲みかけのジュース (*a half-finished juice*). たて = just *completed*; かけ = *not yet completed*. This pair is exactly what the やってみよう tests: パンは焼き**たて**, レポートはまだ書き**かけ**.\n- **V-たばかり** (N3) is a full clause that works with almost any verb and focuses on time: 昨日買ったばかりの傘 (*an umbrella I bought just yesterday*). たて is a fixed suffix focused on the fresh state.\n- **〜次第** (#36) also follows a ます-stem but means *as soon as ~, (I will)…*, not a state.\n\nCommon mistakes:\n- Wrong base: ✗焼くたて, ✗焼いたて → ✓焼きたて.\n- Using it for any recent action: ✗昼ご飯を食べたてだ → ✓食べたばかりだ (*I've just eaten lunch*).\n\nJLPT tip: 問題1 often offers ながら / たて / かけ / しだい after a stem, as in 作り（　）の味. If the food is praised as fresh (ご注文を受けてから作っています), choose たて; if something is left unfinished, かけ.",
