          deepDive: "**Nったら** puts a familiar person or thing in the spotlight in order to complain about it: *honestly, that ~…!* The book: 身近な相手に対して、批判的なことを言いたいときに使う. The tone mixes exasperation with closeness, so the target is someone or something *near* the speaker: family, friends, pets, coworkers, even your own computer: このパソコンったら、しょっちゅうフリーズするんだ (*this computer, I swear, it freezes all the time*).\n\nConnection: **N + ったら** (names, うちの弟, お母さん, このパソコン). It takes the place of the topic は: うちの弟ったら、全部食べちゃったんだよ ≈ 弟は（困ったことに）全部食べちゃった (*my little brother, honestly, ate them all*).\n\nTwo ways to use it:\n- **Talking about** someone: うちの犬ったら、私をよその人と間違えてほえたのよ (*our dog, would you believe it, mistook me for a stranger and barked*).\n- **Talking to** someone, as a gentle scolding: 小林君ったら何言ってるの (sample), お父さんったら、部屋の電気つけっぱなしにしないでよ (Check).\n\nPlus: **Nってば** is used in the same way: お母さんってば、いつも勝手に私の部屋に入るのよ (*Mom, honestly, she's always coming into my room without asking*). ってば sounds a touch more insistent. ってば also has a separate use after a whole sentence, repeating something with irritation: だから、行かないってば！ (*I told you, I'm not going!*). Worth knowing, but it's not what the Plus box teaches.\n\nNuance and register:\n- Casual and emotional (the book marks it with the grumbling and chatting faces). It's especially common in family talk and among women, though anyone can use it.\n- Because it's critical, don't use it to or about a superior in a formal setting. Grumbling うちの社長ってば… to a friend is fine; saying 社長ったら to the president is not.\n- The comment that follows is a complaint, often ending in 〜ちゃった, 〜んだから, 〜のよ.\n\nLook-alikes (not numbered points in this book):\n- The conditional **〜たら**: 雨が降ったら中止 (*if it rains*). ったら here follows a noun directly and expresses a feeling, not a condition.\n- **〜といったら**: *speaking of ~ / when it comes to ~*: 京都といったらお寺だ. Neutral topic-raising.\n- **〜って** (topic): 田中さんって優しいね. Neutral, no criticism.\n\nCommon mistakes:\n- Pure praise: ✗うちの娘ったら、とても優しいんです sounds odd, unless you're mock-complaining (うちの娘ったら、また満点取っちゃって is a humble brag).\n- Strangers as the target: ✗隣の席の知らない人ったら… sounds too intimate.\n\nJLPT tip: when a casual sentence names a person or thing and then complains about what they did, the blank right after the noun is ったら／ってば. In 聴解, ったら tells you the speaker is annoyed with the person named.",
