          deepDive: "**NというN** repeats one noun to mean *every single N, all the N without exception*. The book: 店中の客という客が立ち上がって踊り出した (*every single customer in the place stood up and started dancing*). It's emphatic and a little dramatic, used when the scale is striking: 店という店が閉まっちゃってて、何も買えなかった (*every single shop was closed, so I couldn't buy anything*).\n\nConnection: **N + という + N**, with the **same noun** both times (「〜」の部分は同じ言葉をくり返す): 木という木, 賞という賞, 道という道, 家という家, 食べ放題という食べ放題 (sample: *every single all-you-can-eat place*).\n\nNuance and constraints:\n- The noun is usually concrete and countable: 店, 家, 窓, 道, 木, 賞, 席. Abstract nouns are rare.\n- The predicate describes a complete, sweeping state or event: この辺の木という木は、1本残らず燃えてしまった (*every tree around here burned; not one was left*), 賞という賞を独占した (*swept every award*), 道という道に観光客があふれ. Reinforcers like 全部 or 1本残らず fit; limiters don't: ✗店という店のいくつかが閉まっていた.\n- It takes normal particles after it: 賞という賞を, 道という道に, 家という家が.\n- It's used in conversation and in writing (news, essays, novels), so it isn't strictly casual.\n\nContrast:\n- **N₁というN₂** with *different* nouns (田中という人 *a person called Tanaka*, 「桜」という歌) means *an N₂ called N₁*. Only the repetition gives the \"every\" meaning.\n- **どの〜も / すべての〜**: どの店も閉まっていた is the neutral equivalent; NというN is more vivid.\n- **〜といった** (#64): *such as ~*, giving examples: 寿司や天ぷらといった和食. It lists a few; NというN covers all.\n- **〜というものだ** (#52): *that's what ~ is*, a general verdict. Different structure.\n\nCommon mistakes:\n- Two different nouns: ✗木という植物 does not mean \"every plant.\"\n- A small number: ✗3軒の店という店 contradicts the idea of *all*.\n\nJLPT tip: in 問題2, when the pieces include the same noun twice plus という, they almost certainly form N＋という＋N. In the Check, クリスマスシーズンのこの町は、家＿＿家が電球で飾られて takes という (*every single house is decorated with lights*).",
          see: [64],
          index: ["NというN"],
